  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Today into tonight, a shortwave trough could \nspark another round of showers and storms, but it will be \nworking against a relatively stable boundary layer.\n\nA cold front currently situated across central portions of the \nregion continues to sink southward, and should continue to make \nsouthwestward progress through this morning. This will leave our\nregion in the relatively cooler and more stable air mass north \nof the front. The net result is that our region should be in \nnortheasterly flow, and relatively cloudy conditions for much of\nthe day. As a result, surface based instability is expected to \nbe very limited, with mostly elevated instability present. \n\nConsequently, the risk for severe storms is still quite \nconditional and uncertain. Given how sharp of a mid level short \nwave trough is approaching, there will be ample forcing, but it \nis a question of if the elevated instability will be enough to \nsustain storms in our region.\n\nWith that said, some CAMs do feature storms moving into parts \nof the area late this afternoon and into tonight. Confidence in \ndetails is relatively low, but should the region experience more\ndestabilization than is currently anticipated, storms will be \ncapable of damaging winds, some hail, and perhaps an isolated \ntornado. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across \nthe region on Thursday. \n\nAfter a cool down on Wednesday for most neighborhoods (5 to 10 \ndegrees lower - less across Delmarva), hot and humid conditions are \nonce again expected across the region on Thursday as the frontal \nboundary lifts north. With the front to the north, high temperatures \nare forecast to climb into the upper 80s to mid 90s for many areas \n(around 80 or low 80s far N and W). These temperatures combined with \ndewpoints in the low to mid 70s will again result in heat indices \nwell into the 90s to low 100s south of I-78, perhaps even as high as \n105 degrees for Delmarva.\n\nAnother Heat Advisory may be needed for portions of the area on\nThursday if this trend continues.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled conditions will continue on Thursday \nwith the potential for severe thunderstorms. Scattered \nthunderstorms are again possible on Friday, mainly from \nPhiladelphia south.\n\nAnother round of severe thunderstorms are possible on Thursday. \nFriday could also experience a few severe storms across Delmarva, \ndepending on how quickly the frontal boundary pushes south.\n\nSPC has a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe thunderstorms \non Thursday from about Wilmington to Wildwood NJ southward and a \nMarginal Risk (level 1) to the north of this. \n\nThe northwest flow pattern will remain in place on Thursday and will \ncontinue to be supportive of all severe threats, including damaging \nwinds, large hail, and a few tornadoes, depending on how all the \nmesoscale details pan out. The threat for flash flooding is \ntypically lower with these patterns, but not zero. Training can \nstill occur and a single locale could have multiple days with heavy \nrainfall.\n\nFriday's SPC's 15% probabilities for severe thunderstorms have \nshifted just south of our Delmarva zones, though there is still some \nuncertainty with this. Our latest forecast has about a 30-40% chance \nof showers and thunderstorms south of about Philadelphia.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry \nweather with at or below normal temperatures and lower humidity.\n\nBy Saturday, current model consensus indicates the frontal boundary \nwill be pushing south of our region with Canadian high pressure \nbuilding across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. This will largely \nend any shower and thunderstorm potential. Thus, Labor Day Weekend \nis still shaping up to feature nice weather, with temperatures \ntrending at or slightly below normal and lower humidity. The only \nroad bump at the moment, could be a cold front that approaches the \nforecast area very late on Monday.",
